<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <p class="eyebrow">DSA Notes · Two Pointers</p>
    <h1>Reverse a string, <span class="flip">in place</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">Walk two fingers in from both ends and swap as they pass. My notes on the idea, the one tricky bit, the code, and how to read the constraints.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span class="tag on">Two-pointer</span>
      <span class="tag">In-place · returns void</span>
      <span class="tag">Time O(n)</span>
      <span class="tag">Space O(1)</span>
      <span class="tag">LeetCode 344</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">01</span> The problem, in my words</h2>
    <p class="sub">What am I actually being asked to do?</p>
    <p>I'm given an array of characters and I have to <strong>reverse it</strong> — but not by building a new reversed array. I have to change <em>the same array</em>. That's why the function returns <strong>nothing</strong> (void / <code>None</code>): the "answer" is the array itself, edited where it sits.</p>
    <p>So the whole game is: rearrange the existing slots. First character should end up last, last should end up first, and everything meets in the middle.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">02</span> The idea — two pointers</h2>
    <p class="sub">One finger at the start, one at the end, walking toward each other.</p>
    <p>Put a <span class="lc">left pointer</span> at index <code>0</code> and a <span class="rc">right pointer</span> at the last index. <strong>Swap</strong> the two characters they point to, then step <span class="lc">left</span> forward and <span class="rc">right</span> backward. Repeat.</p>
    <p>I keep going <strong>while <span class="lc">left</span> &lt; <span class="rc">right</span></strong>. The moment they meet or cross, every character has already been moved — so I stop. (In example one the right index starts at <code>3</code>; in example two it starts at <code>6</code>. The rule is the same: stop when the fingers touch.)</p>

    <div class="card viz" id="viz">
      <div class="tiles" id="tiles"></div>
      <div class="swapbar">
        <span id="swaptext">press <b style="font-family:inherit;color:var(--ink)">Step</b> to begin</span>
        <span class="temp" id="temp">temp = <b>–</b></span>
      </div>
      <div class="status" id="status">left = 0 &nbsp; right = 6</div>
      <div class="controls">
        <button class="primary" id="stepBtn">Step ›</button>
        <button id="autoBtn">Auto-play</button>
        <button id="resetBtn">Reset</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">03</span> The twist I found</h2>
    <p class="sub">Why a swap needs a third variable.</p>
    <p>Here's the bit that tripped me up. To swap two slots I can't just do <code>a = b</code> then <code>b = a</code> — the first line <strong>overwrites</strong> <code>a</code>, so the second line just copies the value back and both slots end up the same. The original value of <code>a</code> is gone.</p>
    <div class="note">
      <h4>The fix — a temp variable</h4>
      <p>Stash one value in a <strong>third variable</strong> first, then it's safe to overwrite. <span class="lc">left</span> → <code>temp</code>, then <span class="rc">right</span> → <span class="lc">left</span>, then <code>temp</code> → <span class="rc">right</span>. Nothing is lost. (Python lets you shortcut this with <code>s[l], s[r] = s[r], s[l]</code> — but a hidden temp is doing exactly this behind the scenes.)</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">04</span> The code</h2>
    <p class="sub">Python — both the explicit-temp version and the clean one.</p>
    <div class="codewrap">
      <div class="filerow">
        <span class="dot" style="background:#FF5F56"></span>
        <span class="dot" style="background:#FFBD2E"></span>
        <span class="dot" style="background:#27C93F"></span>
        <span style="margin-left:6px">reverse_string.py</span>
      </div>
<pre><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="fn">Solution</span>:
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">reverseString</span>(self, s: list[str]) -> <span class="kw">None</span>:
        <span class="cm"># modify s in place, return nothing</span>
        left, right = <span class="nu">0</span>, <span class="fn">len</span>(s) - <span class="nu">1</span>

        <span class="kw">while</span> left &lt; right:
            <span class="cm"># --- swap using a temp (the twist) ---</span>
            temp    = s[left]
            s[left] = s[right]
            s[right] = temp

            <span class="cm"># walk the pointers inward</span>
            left  += <span class="nu">1</span>
            right -= <span class="nu">1</span>

        <span class="cm"># Pythonic one-liner for the same swap:</span>
        <span class="cm"># s[left], s[right] = s[right], s[left]</span></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">05</span> Time &amp; space</h2>
    <p class="sub">Solve first, then account for the cost — like I planned.</p>
    <div class="cxrow">
      <div class="cx time">
        <div class="lbl">Time</div>
        <div class="big">O(n)</div>
        <p class="why">I touch each character once. It's really n/2 swaps, but we drop constants — so O(n).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx space">
        <div class="lbl">Space</div>
        <div class="big">O(1)</div>
        <p class="why">Only <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, and one <code>temp</code>. No second array — the size doesn't grow with the input.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">06</span> Reading the constraints</h2>
    <p class="sub">What Big-O means, and how input size hints at the algorithm.</p>
    <p><strong>What does Big-O actually mean?</strong> Think of <code>n</code> as the <strong>size of the input</strong>, and Big-O as roughly <em>how the work grows as n grows</em>. O(n) means "work grows in step with the input"; O(n²) means "double the input, quadruple the work."</p>
    <p>The size limits in a problem are a <strong>hint</strong> about which complexity will survive. Rough guide:</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Input size (n)</th><th>Usually think about</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="size">n ≤ 10</td><td>Almost anything — even <span class="bo r">O(n!)</span> / brute force</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="size">n ≤ 20</td><td>Backtracking / <span class="bo a">exponential</span> may be possible</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="size">n ≤ 100</td><td><span class="bo a">O(n²)</span> often fine</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="size">n ≤ 1,000</td><td><span class="bo a">O(n²)</span> often okay</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="size">n ≤ 10,000</td><td><span class="bo a">O(n²)</span> may be risky</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="size">n ≤ 100,000</td><td>Prefer <span class="bo g">O(n log n)</span> or <span class="bo g">O(n)</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="size">n ≤ 1,000,000</td><td>Usually <span class="bo g">O(n)</span> / <span class="bo g">O(log n)</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">Not strict laws — rough guidelines. When in doubt, aim one tier faster than "just okay."</p>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <span>reverse-string · two pointers</span>
    <span>note to future me →</span>
  </footer>
</div>

<script>
(function () {
  var WORD = "REVERSE".split("");
  var tilesEl = document.getElementById("tiles");
  var tempEl  = document.getElementById("temp");
  var swapTx  = document.getElementById("swaptext");
  var statusEl= document.getElementById("status");
  var stepBtn = document.getElementById("stepBtn");
  var autoBtn = document.getElementById("autoBtn");
  var resetBtn= document.getElementById("resetBtn");

  var chars, left, right, done, phase, autoTimer = null;

  function build() {
    chars = WORD.slice();
    left = 0; right = chars.length - 1; done = false; phase = 0;
    render();
    swapTx.innerHTML = 'press <b style="font-family:inherit;color:var(--ink)">Step</b> to begin';
    tempEl.classList.remove("hot");
    tempEl.innerHTML = "temp = <b>–</b>";
    statusEl.classList.remove("done");
    statusEl.textContent = "left = 0   right = " + (chars.length - 1);
    stepBtn.disabled = false;
  }

  function render() {
    tilesEl.innerHTML = "";
    for (var i = 0; i < chars.length; i++) {
      var cell = document.createElement("div");
      cell.className = "cell";
      var t = document.createElement("div");
      t.className = "tile";
      t.textContent = chars[i];
      if (!done) {
        if (i === left && left < right) t.classList.add("at-left");
        else if (i === right && left < right) t.classList.add("at-right");
        else if (i < left || i > right) t.classList.add("locked");
      } else {
        t.classList.add("locked");
      }
      var p = document.createElement("div");
      p.className = "ptr";
      if (!done && left < right && i === left) { p.classList.add("l"); p.textContent = "▲ L"; }
      else if (!done && left < right && i === right) { p.classList.add("r"); p.textContent = "▲ R"; }
      cell.appendChild(t);
      cell.appendChild(p);
      tilesEl.appendChild(cell);
    }
  }

  function finish() {
    done = true;
    render();
    swapTx.innerHTML = 'reversed → <b style="font-family:Fraunces,serif;color:var(--ink)">' + chars.join("") + '</b>';
    tempEl.classList.remove("hot");
    tempEl.innerHTML = "temp = <b>–</b>";
    statusEl.classList.add("done");
    statusEl.textContent = "left ≥ right — done ✓  (" + chars.join("") + ")";
    stepBtn.disabled = true;
    stopAuto();
  }

  function step() {
    if (done) return;
    if (left >= right) { finish(); return; }
    // do one full swap with temp highlighted
    var tmp = chars[left];
    tempEl.classList.add("hot");
    tempEl.innerHTML = "temp = <b>" + tmp + "</b>";
    swapTx.innerHTML = 'swap <span class="lc">s['+left+']</span> ↔ <span class="rc">s['+right+']</span>';

    var lTile = tilesEl.children[left] && tilesEl.children[left].firstChild;
    var rTile = tilesEl.children[right] && tilesEl.children[right].firstChild;
    if (lTile) lTile.classList.add("pop");
    if (rTile) rTile.classList.add("pop");

    var L = left, R = right;
    setTimeout(function () {
      chars[L] = chars[R];
      chars[R] = tmp;
      left++; right--;
      render();
      if (left < right) {
        statusEl.textContent = "left = " + left + "   right = " + right;
      } else {
        statusEl.textContent = "left = " + left + "   right = " + right + "  (they meet)";
      }
      setTimeout(function(){ if (left >= right && !done) finish(); }, 120);
    }, 320);
  }

  function stopAuto() {
    if (autoTimer) { clearInterval(autoTimer); autoTimer = null; autoBtn.textContent = "Auto-play"; }
  }
  function toggleAuto() {
    if (autoTimer) { stopAuto(); return; }
    if (done) build();
    autoBtn.textContent = "Pause";
    autoTimer = setInterval(function () {
      if (done) { stopAuto(); return; }
      step();
    }, 900);
  }

  stepBtn.addEventListener("click", function () { stopAuto(); step(); });
  autoBtn.addEventListener("click", toggleAuto);
  resetBtn.addEventListener("click", function () { stopAuto(); build(); });

  build();
})();
</script>
